WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.780
In the first film, imatinib won a kinase pocket and escape guided the next drug.

2
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.800
Why did that plan fail for decades against the cancer switch K-Ras?

3
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.880
And how do networks push back even when a drug lands?

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.280
The barrier is binding arithmetic.

5
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.900
A ligand's share of a pocket scales with its concentration divided by its

6
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.180
dissociation constant, the concentration that fills half the sites.

7
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.660
For a typical kinase, 1 mM ATP divided by 10 µM equals 100.

8
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.220
A drug with a 1 nM constant, reaching 1 µM, scores 1,000.

9
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.160
So the drug wins the pocket.

10
00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:50.273
Now run that rule on K-Ras.

11
00:00:51.973 --> 00:00:57.713
Cellular GTP sits near 0.5 mM, while Ras holds it at about 10 pM.

12
00:00:57.713 --> 00:01:02.173
0.5 mM divided by 10 pM is 50,000,000.

13
00:01:03.513 --> 00:01:09.993
A strong reversible drug at 5 µM, with a 1 nM constant, scores 5,000.

14
00:01:11.193 --> 00:01:14.653
That leaves barely 1 Ras in 10,000 holding the drug.

15
00:01:15.683 --> 00:01:18.183
Chemists stopped fighting GTP directly.

16
00:01:19.523 --> 00:01:24.543
One cancer mutation, G12C, replaces glycine 12 with cysteine.

17
00:01:25.743 --> 00:01:29.083
Cysteine exposes a reactive sulphur group called a thiol.

18
00:01:30.403 --> 00:01:36.383
Even mutant K-Ras G12C still slowly cuts GTP to GDP, its off shape.

19
00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:43.077
In that GDP shape, a hidden pocket opens beneath the switch II loop, beside the

20
00:01:43.077 --> 00:01:49.497
nucleotide site. Sotorasib enters and forms a permanent covalent bond to cysteine

21
00:01:49.497 --> 00:01:56.357
12. Normal Ras lacks cysteine 12, so sotorasib spares it and never fights GTP.

22
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.780
Other drugs sidestep natural active pockets too.

23
00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.640
Antibodies grab a receptor's outer face outside the cell.

24
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:11.760
Allosteric inhibitors sit in a side pocket beside an enzyme's active site.

25
00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:16.740
They lock kinases such as MEK shut without displacing ATP.

26
00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:21.073
Yet every drug lands inside a living network.

27
00:02:22.793 --> 00:02:27.693
Remember: sotorasib traps K-Ras G12C only in its brief GDP state.

28
00:02:29.173 --> 00:02:30.173
Pause and predict.

29
00:02:31.253 --> 00:02:36.593
If upstream growth receptors wake up and reload Ras with GTP, what happens to

30
00:02:36.593 --> 00:02:37.593
sotorasib's grip?

31
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:49.140
Reloading GTP shrinks the GDP pool, so fewer Ras molecules get trapped.

32
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.200
Networks push back in other ways.

33
00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:58.340
Block MEK, and ERK drops its braking signal, called negative feedback, so upstream

34
00:02:58.340 --> 00:02:59.460
steps push harder.

35
00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:04.920
Or cells amplify another receptor, like MET, to bypass the block.

36
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.440
That completes the map of targeted drugs.

37
00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:15.240
Side pockets, outer faces, and covalent bonds reach targets that beat reversible

38
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:20.640
competition. Even then, blocking one part ripples through the whole circuit.
